/// \defgroup grp_w1020_gui_gadget_accessors Gadget child accessors and small window members
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief Three small members of GameWindow, WinInstanceData and
///        AnimateWindow; notes on two list box child accessors whose names
///        predate BFME 2's data layout, on the legacy DATA field parser, and
///        on BFME 2's per-cell alignment and per-row selection flag in list
///        box data.
///
/// The legacy window system builds every control (list box, combo box,
/// push button and so on) as a GameWindow with a gadget style and a block
/// of gadget-specific user data (see \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets). Most gadget
/// functions send the gadget a system message (see
/// \ref grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api). The accessors described here
/// do not: they read the gadget's data directly. This page describes:
/// - the child windows a list box creates for itself:
///   GadgetListBoxGetDownButton() and GadgetListBoxGetSlider(), whose names
///   do not match what they return in BFME 2 (see below). Their own
///   entries are listed on the page of the file that declares them,
///   GadgetListBox.cpp, not in this group's function list;
/// - the members in this group's function list: GameWindow::draw, the
///   per-window draw step;
///   WinInstanceData::getTextLength, the length of a window's text; and
///   AnimateWindow::getVel, the motion field of a window animation (a
///   velocity for slides, an angle and a radius for the spiral);
/// - parseData, the parser for the legacy DATA field of a .wnd layout
///   entry. \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers_legacy_data
///   "The legacy DATA field" describes it field by field: the window
///   parser calls it for a DATA keyword, it reads the value by position
///   with strtok and atoi according to the window type, fills one of its
///   own static structures (shared by every call) and points the window's
///   gadget data at it, or at null for an unsupported type, and it always
///   returns true.
///
/// \par Child window accessors and BFME 2's data layouts
/// Zero Hour's GadgetListBox.h and GadgetComboBox.h define small inline
/// functions that return a child window from the gadget's data:
/// GadgetListBoxGetUpButton, GadgetListBoxGetDownButton,
/// GadgetListBoxGetSlider, GadgetComboBoxGetEditBox and others. Each one
/// reads one pointer from the user data and returns it, or null. Because
/// such bodies are identical for any structure with a pointer at the same
/// place, the retail linker merged identical copies into one, and the
/// reconstruction keeps a historical Zero Hour name for each merged copy.
/// BFME 2's list box and combo box data put the children in
/// different places from Zero Hour's, so the names no longer describe the
/// result. The matched BFME 2 callers settle what each copy returns:
/// GameWindowManager::assignDefaultGadgetLook gives the window it gets
/// from GadgetListBoxGetDownButton() the up-button images, the window from
/// GadgetListBoxGetSlider() the down-button images, and the window from
/// GadgetComboBoxGetEditBox the slider images (for a list box) or the
/// push button images of the drop-down button (for a combo box). createGadget,
/// which copies a source gadget's draw data to a new one, pairs them the
/// same way. Port code by role, not by these names.
///
/// \par Cell alignment and the row selection flag
/// BFME 2's list box data carries two settings that Zero Hour's does not.
/// Each list cell has an extra alignment word. BFME 2's W3D list box text
/// drawing (drawListBoxText) reads it for text cells every time it draws:
/// 1 right-aligns the text in its column, 2 centres it, and any other
/// value keeps the default left alignment with a small inset. Image cells
/// ignore it. The list box add handlers set a cell's alignment back to 0
/// whenever they store text or an image in it, so an alignment has to be
/// set again after the cell is refilled.
/// Each row also has a not-selectable flag. The input callbacks test it
/// when a click lands on a row: GadgetListBoxMultiInput (multiple
/// selection) adds nothing to the selection, and GadgetListBoxInput
/// (single selection) selects nothing. In a single-selection list box the
/// click also clears the current selection unless the list box forces a
/// selection. drawListBoxText does not read the flag, so it does not
/// change how the row is drawn. Clearing the list (GLM_DEL_ALL, which
/// GadgetListBoxReset() sends) zeroes every row record, which clears the
/// flag.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Cell alignment and the row flag are not data-driven: the LISTBOXDATA
///   fields of a .wnd layout (see parseListboxData()) carry no per-row or
///   per-cell setting, so only code sets them, at run time.
/// - These are client-side window operations. They do not touch the logic
///   simulation, the logic random numbers, CRC checks, saves or replays.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The list box helpers depend on BFME 2's layouts of the gadget data,
///   which differ from Zero Hour's: the cell and row records are larger
///   (see "Cell alignment and the row selection flag" above) and the child
///   window pointers sit in other places. Code or headers taken from Zero
///   Hour therefore read the wrong fields here; see "Child window
///   accessors" above.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The bodies are BFME 2's; statements about behaviour rest on them and on
/// their matched callers and callees. Statements labelled Zero Hour rest on
/// the Zero Hour source only. parseData's body is ported from the BFME 1
/// reconstruction's window script parser and matches the BFME 2 retail
/// body.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt, \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets,
///      \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists,
///      \ref grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api,
///      \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers


/// \fn GameWindow * GadgetListBoxGetDownButton(GameWindow *listbox)
/// \brief Returns the first of the three scroll children a list box keeps
///        in its data, which in BFME 2 is the up-arrow button, or null.
///
/// Reads one child window pointer from the list box data and returns it,
/// or null when the data is missing. Retail callers include
/// GameWindowManager::assignDefaultGadgetLook and createGadget; both treat
/// the result as the list box's up button.
///
/// \return The child window, or null.
/// \param listbox The list box; must not be null (the data is read
///                through it without a check).
///
/// \warning The name is Zero Hour's and does not describe what BFME 2
///          returns. The body is Zero Hour's inline from GadgetListBox.h,
///          and retail's merged copy at this place serves every function
///          with the same shape. In Zero Hour's list box data this
///          pointer is the down button; BFME 2's list box data, as the
///          matched callers above use it, keeps the up button here. See
///          "Child window accessors" on the group page.
/// \see GadgetListBoxGetSlider()


/// \fn GameWindow * GadgetListBoxGetSlider(GameWindow *listbox)
/// \brief Returns the second of the three scroll children a list box keeps
///        in its data, which in BFME 2 is the down-arrow button, or null.
///
/// Reads the next child window pointer after the one
/// GadgetListBoxGetDownButton() reads, or returns null when the data is
/// missing. Retail callers include GameWindowManager::assignDefaultGadgetLook
/// and createGadget; both treat the result as the list box's down button.
///
/// \return The child window, or null.
/// \param listbox The list box; must not be null.
///
/// \warning The name is Zero Hour's and does not describe what BFME 2
///          returns: in Zero Hour's list box data this pointer is the
///          slider, in BFME 2's it is the down button. The slider itself
///          comes from the merged copy named GadgetComboBoxGetEditBox. As
///          with GadgetListBoxGetDownButton(), the body is Zero Hour's
///          header inline and retail merged identical copies.
/// \see GadgetListBoxGetDownButton()


/// \fn int GameWindow::draw(void *data)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_gui_gadget_accessors
/// \brief Draws the window through its draw callback, if it has one;
///        returns 1 when it did and 0 when it did not.
///
/// The per-window draw step of BFME 2's window system. While drawing the
/// window tree, GameWindowManager::drawWindow calls this virtual for every
/// window that is neither hidden nor see-through, passing the window's
/// instance data. The base version calls the draw callback stored in the
/// window, the one GameWindow::winSetDrawFunc installs (see
/// \ref grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd for draw callbacks named in
/// layouts), with the window and \p data, and returns 1. Without a
/// callback it returns 0, and drawWindow then falls back to a second,
/// object-based drawing path whose shape is not described here.
///
/// \param data The window's instance data, passed through to the callback
///             untouched.
/// \return 1 when a draw callback ran, 0 when the window has none.
///
/// \note Zero Hour has no such virtual: its drawWindow calls the draw
///       callback directly. Making the step virtual lets a derived window
///       class replace it; \ref grp_w1018_gui_checkbox_radio_slider_draw
///       describes gadget look classes whose draw methods do.
/// \note The name is the reconstruction's (the BFME 1 reconstruction
///       declares the same virtual); its virtual-table position, the
///       matched caller and the body establish the role.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This is where the window tree hands drawing to the device layer: the
/// draw callbacks and overrides it reaches include the W3D gadget draw
/// functions. A renderer replacement can keep the window tree and swap
/// what the callbacks and overrides do.
/// \see GameWindowManager::drawWindow, GameWindow::winSetDrawFunc


/// \fn Int WinInstanceData::getTextLength(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_gui_gadget_accessors
/// \brief Returns the length in characters of the window's text, or 0
///        when the window has no text string yet.
///
/// The window's text lives in a display string that the instance data
/// creates on demand (see WinInstanceData). This asks that display string
/// for its length (DisplayString::getTextLength) and returns 0 when none
/// exists. BFME 2's retail callers include GameWindow::winGetTextLength,
/// GameWindow::winSetInstanceData, the gadget factories
/// GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetListBox and
/// GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetComboBox, the list box input and system
/// callbacks, and the W3D push button, check box and radio button draw
/// functions, which draw a label only when the length is non-zero.
///
/// \return The number of characters in the window's text, or 0.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's inline in WinInstanceData.h has the same behaviour;
///       BFME 2 keeps an out-of-line copy that its callers call.
/// \see WinInstanceData, GameWindow::winGetTextLength


/// \fn Coord2D AnimateWindow::getVel(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_gui_gadget_accessors
/// \brief Returns the two-value motion field of a window animation: a
///        velocity for the slide processes, an angle and a radius for the
///        spiral process.
///
/// An AnimateWindow records one window's slide or spiral animation: start,
/// end, current and rest positions, timing and a two-value motion field
/// (named a velocity after its use by the slide processes). The animation
/// processes (ProcessAnimateWindowSlideFromRight,
/// ProcessAnimateWindowSlideFromLeft, ProcessAnimateWindowSlideFromBottom,
/// ProcessAnimateWindowSlideFromTop, ProcessAnimateWindowSlideFromTopFast,
/// ProcessAnimateWindowSlideFromRightFast and ProcessAnimateWindowSpiral)
/// read it in their update, reverse and reverse-setup steps. In the slide
/// steps the matched bodies add the velocity, cut to whole pixels, to the
/// current position on every update call (the start of an animation is
/// delayed by wall-clock time) and then speed it up or slow it down with
/// AnimateWindow::setVel. ProcessAnimateWindowSpiral uses the field
/// differently: x holds an angle and y a radius. Each update places the
/// window at that angle and distance from the animation's end position
/// (radius times cosine and sine of the angle), then advances the angle and
/// shrinks the radius; unless the radius has fallen below half the
/// window's smaller side, which ends the animation, it stores both back
/// with AnimateWindow::setVel. The reverse step turns the other way and
/// grows the radius.
///
/// \return A copy of the field. In the slide processes it is a velocity in
///         display (screen) pixels per update call; the spiral process
///         stores an angle and a radius in it instead.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// In the slide processes each update call moves the window by the
/// velocity, so slide speed is tied to how often the animation manager is
/// updated (Zero Hour: callers of AnimateWindowManager::update include the
/// shell, the control bar, the control bar popup description and the
/// diplomacy screen). Layout positions are scaled to the display
/// resolution when the layout is loaded (parseScreenRect scales each
/// rectangle by the display size over the layout's creation resolution),
/// but the slide processes set and adjust these velocities from fixed
/// values that are not scaled, so at higher resolutions a slide covers a
/// smaller share of the screen per update. A port that changes the update
/// rate or the resolution changes a slide's apparent speed unless it
/// rescales the velocities.
/// \note The name is Zero Hour's (AnimateWindowManager.h declares
///       AnimateWindow::getVel), carried through the BFME 1
///       reconstruction; BFME 2's callers and the field they read confirm
///       the role.
/// \see AnimateWindow::setVel
